Bhai badhiya! 🎉 Saare 6 models ban gaye. Ab DbContext banate hain — yeh class hi EF Core ko batati hai ki kaunse models kaunse tables se map honge.

Step: ApplicationDbContext.cs banao

Models folder ke bahar — project root mein right-click → Add → Class → naam: ApplicationDbContext.cs

csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace MercatoPro.Models   // AdminApp mein "AdminApp.Models" likhna
{
    public class ApplicationDbContext : DbContext
    {
        public ApplicationDbContext(DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext> options)
            : base(options)
        {
        }

        public DbSet<User> Users { get; set; }
        public DbSet<Category> Categories { get; set; }
        public DbSet<Product> Products { get; set; }
        public DbSet<Cart> Carts { get; set; }
        public DbSet<Order> Orders { get; set; }
        public DbSet<OrderItem> OrderItems { get; set; }
    }
}
Yeh class dono projects (MercatoPro aur AdminApp) mein banani hai — same pattern, sirf namespace change.

Next step — Program.cs mein DbContext register karna hai

Program.cs file open karo dono projects mein aur yeh line add karo (var app = builder.Build(); se pehle):

csharp
builder.Services.AddDbContext<ApplicationDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));
Bhai pehle ApplicationDbContext.cs bana le dono projects mein, phir Program.cs dikhao mujhe — exact line kahan add karni hai woh point out kar dunga. 🙂

ApplicationDbContext me dono me add kiya abhi next builder.Services.AddDbContext<ApplicationDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

