Jun 21
Bhai yeh problem aayi kyunki AdminApp ka separate Migrations folder hai jo "fresh start" se migration banane ki koshish kar raha hai — usko apna __EFMigrationsHistory record nahi mila (kyunki pehle migration MercatoPro se chalaya tha, AdminApp se nahi), isliye usne sab tables phir se create karne ki koshish ki — including Categories jo already exist karta hai (kyunki MercatoPro ne pehle hi bana diya tha).

Important concept samajh bhai:

Tune 2 alag DbContext banaye hain (AdminApp ka apna, MercatoPro ka apna), but same database use kar rahe ho. EF Core har project ka apna __EFMigrationsHistory track karta hai uske apne Migrations folder ke basis pe — yeh dono independent hain, ek dusre ko nahi pata doosre ne kya banaya already.

Isiliye jab AdminApp se migration chalaya, usko pata hi nahi tha ki Categories table already MercatoPro ne bana diya hai — usne phir se try kiya banane ka, aur fail ho gaya.

Fix — Sirf Column Alter Karna Hai, Poori Migration Nahi
Bhai simplest fix yeh hai — directly SQL Server mein jaake column ko manually NULL allow karwa do, migration ka jhanjhat chhodo abhi:

SSMS mein yeh query chalao:

sql
ALTER TABLE Products
ALTER COLUMN ImageUrl NVARCHAR(255) NULL;
Phir AdminApp ka pending migration remove kar do (jo fail hui thi):

Package Manager Console mein (Default project = AdminApp):

bash
Remove-Migration
MercatoPro ka migration already successful hua tha (pehla try), woh rakh sakte ho — koi issue nahi.

Bhai pehle SSMS mein ALTER TABLE query chala, phir AdminApp mein Remove-Migration kar — confirm kar dono ho gaye toh submit try karte hain product add karna. 🙂

